{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\chuanle song\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Microsoft\\\\WindowsApps\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content pipeline/analyses/AdaRES-F1_mask_uncertainty_diagnosis_20260924-144300.md -Raw; Get-Content pipeline/analyses/AdaRES-F2_geometry_diagnosis_20260924-144631.md -Raw; Get-Content pipeline/analyses/AdaRES-H1-H4_negative_diagnoses_20260925-173300.md -Raw'","aggregated_output":"# AdaRES-F1 mask-uncertainty diagnosis\n\n2026-09-24 14:43 Asia/Shanghai. Read-only B4-E30 best-checkpoint diagnostic on\nserver-2 GPU1, fixed first 16 ScanRefer validation scenes (441 expressions).\nNo training, deployment of model weights, or change to D1.\n\nThe proposed F1 signal was mean binary entropy of each predicted mask over\nvalid superpoints. The predeclared diagnostic asked whether the missed oracle\nhad *lower* entropy than the selected query in B4's material selection errors.\nGround truth identified oracle queries for analysis only.\n\nOf 240 errors with oracle IoU minus selected IoU > 0.05, just 86 had a\nlower-entropy oracle. Mean entropy was 0.00446 for selected queries and\n0.00632 for oracles. Choosing the minimum-entropy query without labels\ngave subset mIoU 0.0262 versus B4's original 0.4919. The evidence\ncontradicts the stated selection mechanism in this bounded subset.\n\nF1 is **rejected at diagnosis**. No F1 model, config, admission receipt or\ntraining job was created. A different entropy definition chosen by scanning\nvalidation results would violate the project's no-feature-tuning rule.\nThe 16-scene result cannot prove that uncertainty never helps other tasks.\n\nRaw evidence: `pipeline/analyses/AdaRES-F1_mask_uncertainty_16scenes_20260924.json`.\nNative probe: `AdaRES/tools/diagnose_adares_f1_mask_uncertainty.py`.\n\r\n# AdaRES-F2 spatial compactness diagnosis\n\n2026-09-24 14:46 Asia/Shanghai. B4-E30 best checkpoint, first 16 ScanRefer\nvalidation scenes, 441 expressions, read-only native probe on server-2 GPU1.\nGround truth was used only to identify the oracle query after prediction.\n\nThe predeclared signal was binary-foreground spatial variance around the\npredicted-mask centroid, divided by the scene bounding-box squared diagonal,\non valid superpoints. Smaller values indicate a more compact mask. Among\n240 material selection errors (oracle IoU - selected IoU > 0.05), all pairs\nwere nonempty and only 118 oracles were more compact. Mean normalized\nvariance: selected 0.00241258; oracle 0.00237760. This is essentially\nbalanced and does not support the predicted direction.\n\nF2 is rejected at diagnosis: no model implementation, native backward,\nresource check, admission, queue entry, or training launch. The bounded\nsubset does not disprove every geometric mechanism. A new compactness\ndefinition selected after inspecting these validation results would be\nfeature scanning, prohibited by the goal.\n\nEvidence: `pipeline/analyses/AdaRES-F2_geometry_16scenes_20260924.json`\nand `AdaRES/tools/diagnose_adares_f2_geometry.py`. D1 on GPU0 was not\nmodified. GPU1 remains idle pending an independently justified candidate.\n\r\n# Independent route screens after G1\n\n2026-09-25 17:33 Asia/Shanghai. Read-only fixed first 16 ScanRefer\nvalidation scenes, B4-E30 best checkpoint. These are diagnostic subset\nresults, not population estimates or validation-selected hyperparameters.\n\n| Route | Fixed question | Observation | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| H1 | Does 128-query sampling omit target-bearing seeds available among 256 FPS seeds? | 0/240 material errors had available-but-unsampled target seeds. 27/441 had no target-bearing FPS seed; their oracle mask IoU averaged 0.803. | Reject sampler-omission mechanism; no query-budget scan. |\n| H2 | Does a budget-preserving four-per-query first-layer fine shortlist improve target coverage? | Material-error coverage 0.70936 original versus 0.70842 balanced; 12 improved, 35 worsened. Later original layers already cover >0.89. | Reject this redistribution; no alternative k search. |\n| H3 | Does B4's separately trained mask-quality score head provide a useful standalone selection signal? | Score-only mIoU 0.38265 versus indicator 0.49186; among 240 material errors 40 improved and 44 worsened. | Reject standalone score-head reranking; no validation weight scan. |\n| H4 | Are relation-bearing expressions enriched among B4 selection errors? | 204/387 relation-marked material errors versus 36/54 unmarked (52.7% versus 66.7%). | Reject simple relation-gated successor; cohort is small and observational. |\n\nH5 is independent of H1-H4: it adds a final all-query message rather than\nchanging sampler top-k, fine shortlist, score weighting or relation gates.\nIts own diagnosis and gates are recorded in the H5 review and receipt.\n\nRaw paired evidence: `AdaRES-H1_sampling_16scenes_20260925.json`,\n`AdaRES-H2_fine_16scenes_20260925.json`,\n`AdaRES-H3_score_16scenes_20260925.json`,\n`AdaRES-H4_relation_16scenes_20260925.json`.\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
